7:Abstract Our world is open-ended, non-stationary, and constantly evolving; thus what we 1 talk about and how we talk about it change over time. This inherent dynamic nature 2 of language contrasts with the current static language modelling paradigm, which 3 trains and evaluates models on utterances from overlapping time periods. Despite 4 impressive recent progress, we demonstrate that Transformer-XL language models 5 perform worse in the realistic setup of predicting future utterances from beyond 6 their training period, and that model performance becomes increasingly worse with 7 time. We ﬁnd that, while increasing model size alone—a key driver behind recent 8 progress—does not solve this problem, having models that continually update their 9 knowledge with new information can indeed mitigate this performance degradation 10 over time. Hence, given the compilation of ever-larger language modelling datasets, 11 combined with the growing list of language-model-based NLP applications that 12 require up-to-date factual knowledge about the world, we argue that now is the right 13 time to rethink the static way in which we currently train and evaluate our language 14 models, and develop adaptive language models that can remain up-to-date with 15 respect to our ever-changing and non-stationary world. We will publicly release 16 our dynamic, streaming language modelling benchmarks for WMT and AR X IV to 17 facilitate language model evaluation that takes temporal dynamics into account. 18
13: In recent years, substantial efforts in neural language modelling have focused on ﬁnding better neural 20 architectures, building increasingly larger models, and compiling ever-larger amounts of training 21 data, which have been shown to endow language models with the ability to perform well on a wide 22 variety of downstream tasks with minimal ﬁne-tuning (Vaswani et al., 2017; Radford et al., 2019; 23 Brown et al., 2020). While this approach has led to impressive progress, it nevertheless relies on a 24 static experimental paradigm. Concretely, the prevailing practice is to curate a large pretraining web 25 crawl—randomly partitioned into a training set and a validation set in a time-agnostic fashion—and 26 then evaluate on tasks and benchmarks that mostly overlap in time with the pretraining data. 1 27 In this work, we argue that such practices carry two potential risks. First, they do not assess a language 28 model’s ability to generalize well to future data from beyond their training period—an important 29 ability we henceforth refer to as temporal generalization . In our dynamic and non-stationary 30 world, temporal generalization is a key necessity: Many practical machine learning systems that use 31 language model (LM) pretraining, such as machine translation and dialogue systems, are deployed on 32 utterances that users will say in the future, whilst being trained on utterances that users have already 33 1 In the case of GPT-3 (Brown et al., 2020), such tasks include LAMBADA (Paperno et al., 2016), TriviaQA (Joshi et al., 2017b), and WMT translation datasets, among others. These tasks were introduced between 2014 and 2017, which overlap in time with the GPT-3 CommonCrawl dataset that covered the period of 2016-2019. Submitted to 35th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2021). Do not distribute. said in the past. Furthermore, temporal generalization is also crucial to perform well on realistic 34 use cases of language models in the real world. Examples include ﬂagging fake news about recent 35 events that happen outside of the training period (Thorne and Vlachos, 2018; Zellers et al., 2019; 36 Augenstein et al., 2019), forecasting stock prices from the latest news articles (Ding et al., 2015), and 37 answering knowledge-intensive questions like “How many people have been infected by COVID-19?” 38 and “Has the USA ever had a female Vice President?”, whose answers have evolved with time. 39 Second, the temporal overlap between the training and evaluation data increases the risk of “test data 40 contamination”, where parts of the evaluation task are unwittingly included in the pretraining data. 41 Indeed, many language modelling evaluations treat the data as independent and identically distributed 42 (i.i.d) at either the sentence (Chelba et al., 2013) or document level (Brown et al., 2020; Gao et al., 43 2021). Nevertheless, language modelling data are not i.i.d. (neither at the word, sentence, or document 44 level); rather it is a time series, and thus models trained on the preﬁx of a sample from the series 45 should be evaluated on the continuation of that series. While previous research (Levenberg et al., 46 2010) has highlighted the importance of temporal splits for fairer and more realistic evaluations—and 47 has led to research (Osborne et al., 2014; Yogatama et al., 2014) that addresses language modelling 48 from this streaming perspective (§7)—using temporal splits (or splits beyond random ones) is still 49 the exception rather than the rule, as evidenced by many contemporary LM (Brown et al., 2020; Gao 50 et al., 2021) and downstream tasks (Lewis et al., 2020a) that are affected by test data contamination. 2 51 Here we begin with our ﬁrst question: To what extent does the current static language modelling 52 practice overestimate performance, compared to the more realistic setup that evaluates LMs on future 53 utterances? To this end, we introduce our dynamic, streaming language modelling benchmarks (§2), 54 and ﬁnd that Transformer-XLs (Dai et al., 2019) perform up to 16% worse when predicting articles 55 that are published up to 2 years after the end of the training period. Moreover, model performance 56 becomes increasingly worse with time (§3). Given this ﬁnding, we ask: What kinds of predictions is 57 the model struggling with in the dynamic evaluation setup?—-which we answer in §3.1. 58 Beyond LM perplexity evaluation, we further ask: How exactly does this temporal performance 59 degradation of Transformer LMs manifest in different types of question-answering (QA) tasks? We 60 answer this through two different QA tasks, including one around recent events happening outside 61 of the LM training period (§5). Lastly, given the challenges presented by temporal generalization 62 for LMs: What, then, is the remedy? This question is important because keeping LMs up-to-date by 63 retraining with new data is expensive in compute and carbon costs (Strubell et al., 2019; Patterson 64 et al., 2021), and risks the model getting outdated in-between long retraining cycles. 3 We ﬁnd that 65 increasing model size alone—a key driver behind recent LM progress (Kaplan et al., 2020)—is 66 not a solution for the temporal generalization problem (§4): Larger models suffer from the same 67 performance degradation with time, and a smaller model trained on more recent data can 68 outperform a 60% larger model that lacks access to more recent data . We then explore a simple 69 yet effective way of keeping our models up-to-date by continually updating the model’s parameters 70 through dynamic evaluation (Mikolov et al., 2010; Krause et al., 2019), which performs a few steps 71 of gradient descent on streams of new data (§6), and outline other promising approaches in this 72 direction (§7). We conclude with the following recommendations for future LM research: 73 • We should evaluate LMs on their generalization ability to future data, which circumvents test data 74 contamination, rewards models that generalize beyond the surface patterns of their pretraining data, 75 and better reﬂects how large LMs are used in practical systems. We thus argue for the broader 76 inclusion of timestamp information in pretraining data and downstream tasks to make this possible. 77 • Stale LMs that are deployed far outside of their training period perform substantially worse on 78 downstream tasks that require up-to-date factual knowledge, although a broader set of experiments 79 are needed to pinpoint what kinds of tasks are most affected. Our ﬁndings also highlight the need 80 for more tasks, benchmarks, and metrics that evaluate how well and how rapidly LMs are able to 81 integrate new information, which are important ingredients to encourage progress in this direction. 82 • All in all, above and beyond impressive scaling efforts towards ever-larger models (Brown et al., 83 2020; Fedus et al., 2021), we argue for the development of adaptive language models that can 84 remain up-to-date with respect to our open-ended and non-stationary world. 85 2 Brown et al. (2020) used n -gram ﬁltering and deduplication to remove overlaps between the training and test sets. This can potentially induce a correlation between the training and evaluation sets that LMs can exploit. 3 These risks are exacerbated by the trend of ever-larger LMs, where retraining incurs even higher costs. 2
19:2.1 Datasets 89
21: We identify news and scientiﬁc articles as two sources of dynamic streaming data with a naturally 90 changing distribution over time—lending themselves well to evaluating how well language models 91 generalize over time. For the scientiﬁc domain, we use the publicly available arXiv abstracts 92 ( AR X IV ). 4 For news, we use the publicly available WMT News Crawl ( WMT ). 4 We ensure that any 93 trends we observe also generalize well to models trained on larger datasets—which reliably improve 94 language modelling and downstream task performance (Liu et al., 2019)—by compiling a larger 95 news corpus that we term C USTOM N EWS . This dataset consists of crawled English news sources 96 from 1969-2019, and covers various topics including politics, ﬁnance, and sport. We apply minimal 97 preprocessing through: (i) Removal of non-English documents, (ii) deduplication using the MinHash 98 algorithm, and (iii) tokenization using Moses. 4 Table 1 summarizes key statistics of our datasets. 99 2.2 Experiment: A model up to 2 years stale 100 Evaluation period and test set. For each dataset, we pick the last two years (i.e. 2018 and 2019) 101 as our evaluation period, and sub-sample a test set of 24k test documents (1k per test month). 102 TIME - STRATIFIED setup. In this setup, we evaluate LMs trained on the past based on their ability 103 to predict future articles that are published after the time period of their training data; this split is 104 constructed using the time stamp of each article. Here we use all documents from the beginning of 105 each dataset’s time period up until September 2017 as training data, and use the last three months of 106 2017 as our validation period; we denote this as the TIME - STRATIFIED setup. We then evaluate the 107 model on the 2018-2019 test set above, which evaluates the model’s ability to generalize across time 108 by predicting articles up to two years after the end of their training period—a realistic time frame 109 during which we expect large-scale language models to be used without retraining on recent data. 110 CONTROL setup. We assess whether time stratiﬁcation poses a challenge for current LMs by 111 comparing it with the following CONTROL setup. In this setup, the training set includes documents 112 that come from the same 2018-2019 period as the evaluation set (naturally excluding the test docu- 113 ments themselves). This CONTROL setup thus resembles the prevailing (static) language modelling 114 experimental practices, which train and evaluate LMs on text data from overlapping time periods. 115 Crucially, we control such that the two training sets are of the exact same size , i.e., they differ only 116 in the time periods of their training data, rather than in their absolute training set sizes. Here we 117 construct the CONTROL training data by taking the most recent documents starting from the end of the 118 evaluation period (excluding the test documents and including the same number of training documents 119 per test month), and keep adding documents from previous time periods until we reach the same 120 training size as the TIME - STRATIFIED setup. In Table 1, we report the proportion of documents in the 121 CONTROL setup’s training data that come from the same 2018-2019 time period as the evaluation 122 set, which is higher for AR X IV and C USTOM N EWS due to their recent exponential growth of new 123 documents. We sample a similarly-sized validation set as the TIME - STRATIFIED setup, which in this 124 case comes from the 2018-2019 evaluation period (again excluding the test documents). Importantly, 125 both the TIME - STRATIFIED and CONTROL models are evaluated on the exact same test set from the 126 2018-2019 period, which facilitates a fair perplexity comparison between the two setups. 127 Relative perplexity comparison. We want to measure temporal degradation , i.e. do Transformer 128 LMs perform increasingly worse when predicting test documents further into the future? However, 129 4 ArXiv: https://arxiv.org/help/oa/index ; WMT News: http://data.statmt.org/ news-crawl ; and SacreMoses: https://github.com/alvations/sacremoses . 3 any absolute perplexity degradation of the TIME - STRATIFIED model over time (e.g., perplexity for 130 Jan. 2018 vs Dec. 2018) is an unreliable measure: Some months have longer documents, which 131 lead to higher perplexity. We thus measure temporal degradation through relative perplexity changes 132 between the TIME - STRATIFIED and CONTROL models for the same test month (e.g. Dec. 2018). 133
29: 144 To what extent does the static CON - 145 TROL setup overestimate model perfor- 146 mance, compared to the more realistic 147 TIME - STRATIFIED setup that evaluates LMs 148 on future utterances? Fig. 2 presents the 149 results of our ﬁrst experiment. Although we 150 train both models: (i) On the exact same dataset 151 sizes, and (ii) using the same model architec- 152 tures, a stale TIME - STRATIFIED model performs 153 worse than the CONTROL model, which has 154 seen training data from the test period—with 155 up to 16% perplexity difference. We attribute 156 the higher relative degradation on C USTOM N EWS and AR X IV to their recent exponential growth 157 of new documents, resulting in a higher proportion of documents from the test period in the data 158 (Table 1), hence presenting a more difﬁcult temporal generalization problem. 159 Do Transformer LMs perform increasingly 160 worse when predicting future utterances fur- 161 ther away from their training period? To 162 this end, Fig. 1 plots the relative perplexity in- 163 crease of the TIME - STRATIFIED over the CON - 164 TROL model. As evidenced by the upward slope 165 on all datasets, the model deteriorates more as 166 we ask it to predict data further away from the 167 training period, afﬁrming that the model indeed 168 becomes increasingly outdated with time. 169 How general are these ﬁndings? We ﬁnd that 170 the same patterns not only generalize across 171 datasets, as we have just shown, but are also 172 found: (i) For test years other than 2018-2019 173 (Appendix A.1), (ii) beyond the two-year tempo- 174 ral gap between the end of the training and test 175 periods (Appendix A.2), and (iii) across other 176 languages (German WMT, Appendix A.3). 177
37: 207 In practice, adaptation is a key necessity to max- 208 imize the potential of LMs in our dynamic and 209 non-stationary world. This includes the ability 210 to integrate information about new words and 211 concepts that never occurred in the past, and 212 also words whose context or meaning had sub- 213 stantially changed across time. This need is well-reﬂected in our datasets: About 27% of word types 214 (i.e. unique words) on C USTOM N EWS each month had never occurred in the training period, such 215 as “Brexiteers” and “MeToo”. We refer to these as EMERGING NEW WORDS , and argue that these 216 concepts are important because they reﬂect precisely the dynamic nature of our non-stationary world. 217 Perhaps the most notable recent EMERGING NEW WORDS is “COVID-19”, which had zero unigram 218 probability prior to late-2019, and yet constitutes an important use case of the NLP systems today. 219 on EMERGING NEW WORDS on WMT , broken down by whether the word is encountered for the 1st or the 2nd time in the test document, and for the latter, whether the 1st occurrence was in the TXL context. The last column shows results with dynamic evaluation (§6). Concretely, we deﬁne EMERGING NEW 220 WORDS as those that occur frequently on the 221 test set (at least 50 times), but either: (i) were 222 previously unseen on the training set, or (ii) 223 occurred much less frequently on the training 224 set than on the test set, as indicated by an at 225 least 5 times lower unigram probability. This 226 procedure yields a reasonably-sized set of 287 227 EMERGING NEW WORDS and 87,636 mentions 228 in our 2018-2019 test documents. Many of 229 these words indeed reﬂect strong temporal 230 dynamics: e.g. “Ardern” (who became the 231 New Zealand PM in late-2017) and “Novichok” 232 (which is what Sergey and Yulia Skripal 233 were poisoned with in 2018). Fig. 3 shows 234 that the TIME - STRATIFIED model performs 235 substantially worse for EMERGING NEW 236 WORDS —an almost 5x worse perplexity (110 237 vs 22) than the overall one (Fig. 2). 238 Perplexity of ﬁrst and second occurrences of EMERGING NEW WORDS . We now ask: How well 239 can Transformer LMs rapidly adapt to new information and EMERGING NEW WORDS ? Concretely, 240 5 LMs that perform well in our non-stationary world should be able to predict subsequent occurrences 241 of EMERGING NEW WORDS (e.g. “COVID-19”), which exhibit strong temporal dynamics, much 242 better than the ﬁrst occurrences of these words, because these words appear frequently on the test set 243 preﬁx—even though these EMERGING NEW WORDS do not appear as frequently on the training set. 244 In Fig. 3, we show the perplexity obtained by the TIME - STRATIFIED model under two conditions: 245 For the ﬁrst and second occurrences of EMERGING NEW WORDS in a test document. 246 Although the model has a high ppl. the ﬁrst time it generates EMERGING NEW WORDS in the document 247 (ppl. of ∼ 694.95), it has a much lower ppl. for generating the same words for the second time, but 248 only if the ﬁrst word is available in the Transformer context. In such case, the model can simply 249 copy the same word from the context; this ﬁnding reafﬁrms the strong copying ability of the attention 250 block (Bahdanau et al., 2015; Vinyals et al., 2015). This means that the ability of Transformers to 251 condition on long-range context is already a useful feature for temporal generalization, even when we 252 are not explicitly updating the model parameters with new data. However, we observe no such effect 253 when the ﬁrst occurrence falls outside of the Transformer memory (ppl. of >2,700), highlighting the 254 need to scale Transformers to even longer sequences (Child et al., 2019; Correia et al., 2019; Kitaev 255 et al., 2020; Beltagy et al., 2020, inter alia ) to improve temporal generalization. 256 Importance. Our analyses provide a targeted evaluation of temporal generalization in LMs, which 257 enable us to benchmark progress precisely on things that matter the most for temporal generalization 258 (e.g. evaluating LMs on named entities, fast-changing topics, and adaptation speed to EMERGING 259 NEW WORDS , rather than relying on overall ppl. as a sole metric for measuring LM progress). 260
41: STRATIFIED models with 287M (dotted line) and 448M parameters (solid line), respectively, over the CONTROL model with 287M parameters, for WMT and C USTOM N EWS (§4). Recently, increasing model size has led to sub- 262 stantial improvements in perplexity, downstream 263 tasks, and few-shot learning ability (Kaplan 264 et al., 2020; Brown et al., 2020). But can in- 265 creasing model size also improve temporal gen- 266 eralization? To this end, we train a bigger TIME - 267 STRATIFIED model with 448M parameters—a 268 60% increase over the previous 287M model 269 and 30% larger than GPT-2 MEDIUM . 270 Similar to Section 3, we report the respec- 271 tive perplexity increase of the newly trained 272 TIME - STRATIFIED 448 M model over the CON - 273 TROL 287 M model (solid lines). We reproduce 274 the relative perplexity increase of the smaller 275 TIME - STRATIFIED 287 M model over the CON - 276 TROL 287 M one (Fig. 2) as the dotted lines. 277 If increasing the model size was able to delay 278 temporal degradation, we would expect to see 279 the solid lines produced by the bigger models 280 to have reduced (i.e., ﬂatter) slopes compared to the dotted lines produced by the smaller models. 281 While larger TIME - STRATIFIED models, as expected, achieve lower absolute perplexities (5.5% 282 improvement), model size has no signiﬁcant effect on the slope of these lines ( p > 0 . 05 , assessed 283 using a t-test on the slopes found by ﬁtting a linear regression). On both datasets, by the end of the 284 test period (i.e. late-2019), a smaller but more up-to-date CONTROL 287 M model outperforms 285 a 60% larger but two-year out-of-date TIME - STRATIFIED 448 M model . Hence, building models 286 that perform well in this setup requires solutions that more directly tackle the speciﬁc challenges we 287 emphasized through our ﬁndings so far, and update the model’s knowledge with new information. 288
45: 289 So far we have evaluated the LMs intrinsically, through perplexity, which is important because 290 language modelling is a foundational task that affects many NLP applications through language 291 model pretraining. However, we still do not know how this perplexity deterioration affects practical 292 applications of LMs, i.e., how do out-of-date LMs affect different types of downstream tasks? 293 6 we ask questions (i ) error Closed-book question answering (QA). 294 Closed-book QA is a popular test bed for 295 evaluating pretrained LMs, which have to 296 compress and encode knowledge found in 297 a big corpus. But given the relative lack of 298 existing time-stamped, news QA datasets that evaluate LMs’ ability to answer questions about 300 events that happen outside of their training 301 period in a closed-book fashion, we construct 302 a dataset of synthetic questions on government 303 ofﬁcials using the following template: “ Who 304 is the [government role] of [country/state] in 305 [month/year]? ” In total, we construct a test set 306 of 438 questions about 22 different government 307 roles from 11 countries (see Appendix C for 308 examples). We pretrain the TXL model (as 309 described in Section 2.3) using the WMT 310 dataset up to the years 2012, 2013,..., 2019, 311 respectively. We then ﬁne-tune these models to 312 answer questions about government ofﬁcials for 313 2011 to get the model accustomed to the task format, and evaluate on synthetic questions related 314 to the year 2019. Fig. 4 shows the substantial accuracy deterioration as we shift the end of the 315 pretraining data away from 2019, the year for which we ask questions. This ﬁnding demonstrates how 316 the ﬁne-tuned LMs’ lack of more recent factual knowledge affects their performance on this task. 6 317 Reading comprehension. Nevertheless, we do not expect all downstream tasks to be equally 318 affected by outdated LMs. To illustrate this point, we perform a reading comprehension experiment 319 using NewsQA (Trischler et al., 2017), where the evidence documents are presented together with 320 the questions into the preﬁx of the model. Hence, the model has all necessary information to answer 321 the questions, and thus outdated LMs will likely present less of a challenge in this type of tasks. We 322 obtain a TIME - STRATIFIED NewsQA by recovering the articles’ timestamps. 7 . We test on questions 323 from 2009, for a of total 10000 questions (see Appendix C for question-answer examples). We 324 evaluate how well LMs trained on C USTOM N EWS until the end of 2008 performs in comparison to 325 LMs trained until the end of 2009: Both models perform identically at 0.47 F1. Hence, time-stratiﬁed 326 evaluations for reading comprehension, where the answers are extractive and can be copied from the 327 passage, pose less of a challenge for outdated LMs, unlike knowledge-intensive, closed-book QA. 328
49: 329 One way to mitigate LMs’ degradation over time 330 is to continually update the models’ knowledge 331 with new information as new documents arrive 332 into the stream. One of the ways to do this is 333 through dynamic evaluation (Mikolov et al., 334 2010; Graves, 2013; Krause et al., 2019)—a 335 form of online learning that continually updates 336 the parameters of a pretrained model by perform- 337 ing gradient descent on the new data. While 338 most prior work used dynamic evaluation to per- 339 form updates within a document, hence adapting 340 to local topical shifts, here we use it to adapt 341 to the temporal dynamics that occur within a 342 stream of chronologically ordered documents, 343 hence adapting to temporal trends across docu- 344 ments. Appendix B has more details on dynamic 345 evaluation and our empirical settings. 346 6 We analyzed the slight accuracy drop in 2019 compared to 2018, and this is because the 2019 model mixes up the names of Russian and American presidents, which co-occurred in the same context during this period. 7 https://cs.nyu.edu/ kcho/DMQA/ 7 Parametersthatgetupdated We plot the results in Fig. 5: Dotted lines 347 reﬂect the perplexity increase when com- paring the CONTROL model to the TIME - 349 STRATIFIED model, i.e., the same graph as 350 in Fig. 1, whereas solid lines reﬂect the per- 351 plexity increase achieved when comparing 352 the same CONTROL model with the TIME - 353 STRATIFIED model augmented with dynamic 354 evaluation ( TIME - STRATIFIED dyn ). In all 355 datasets, dynamic evaluation reduces the speed of the model becoming outdated, as evidenced 356 by the reduced upward slope, with a signiﬁcant effect for AR X IV and WMT ( p < 0 . 05 , assessed us- 357 ing a t-test on the slopes found by ﬁtting a linear regression). The improvements are more pronounced 358 for AR X IV , where a more granular analysis over weeks reveals that the model needs only about one 359 week worth of data to overtake the CONTROL model. Moreover, we see much larger improvements 360 for predicting EMERGING NEW WORDS , which exhibit strong temporal dynamics (§3.1, see Fig. 3): 361 We observe a 39.62% ppl. reduction from 109.73 to 66.2 for EMERGING NEW WORDS , compared to 362 the overall ppl. reduction (a 1.25% reduction from 22.45 to 22.17 for WMT ; Fig. 4). 363 When aiming to keep models up-to-date (especially for larger models), lightweight yet effective 364 approaches are preferable because they allow the model to rapidly digest new information with 365 minimal time, computation, and carbon costs. We thus experiment with updating only the embedding 366 layer (i.e., 52M parameters), capturing lexical semantic changes, as well as updating only the bias 367 terms at all layers (i.e., 198K parameters), as recently introduced by Ben-Zaken et al. (2021). Fig. 4 368 presents the results: In line with the ﬁndings of Ben-Zaken et al. (2021), updating only the bias terms 369 performs nearly as well as updating the full model. 370 Beyond dynamic evaluation We remark that dynamic evaluation alone, while effective, does not 371 fully solve temporal generalization, as evidenced by the prevailing (albeit gentler) upward slopes on 372 WMT and C USTOM N EWS . We expect that larger gains can be achieved by fully embracing continual 373 learning in LMs—striking a balance between quickly adapting to new data and achieving positive 374 forward transfer (Lopez-Paz and Ranzato, 2017), while avoiding catastrophic forgetting(Mccloskey 375 and Cohen, 1989; Kirkpatrick et al., 2017). Furthermore, recent semi-parametric models (Guu et al., 376 2020; Lewis et al., 2020b; Karpukhin et al., 2020; Khandelwal et al., 2020; Yogatama et al., 2021) lend 377 themselves well to continual learning, where new knowledge can be stored in an external memory, 378 which can be updated without retraining the whole model. A related approach is to disentangle the 379 acquisition of up-to-date knowledge from the language learning itself, and enable direct editing of 380 that knowledge within the model (Sinitsin et al., 2020; Zhu et al., 2020; De Cao et al., 2021). 381
57: We evaluated the extent to which our current language models can generalize well in the realistic 423 setup of predicting future utterances outside their training period. We found that current practices that 424 train and evaluate models on data from overlapping time period overestimate model generalization 425 to future utterances, and that Transformer LMs become increasingly outdated with time. We found 426 that increasing model size alone—a key driver behind recent LM success—is not a solution for this 427 problem, and that this degradation affects downstream tasks that require up-to-date factual knowledge. 428 Generality to other domains. The importance of temporal generalization extends beyond language 429 modelling and NLP. Many other commercial machine learning systems like speech processing and 430 video understanding also involve non-stationary data, and are similarly trained on data that were 431 collected in the past, but deployed on new data from the future. Since many of these tasks also use 432 Transformers (Girdhar et al., 2019; Gulati et al., 2020, inter alia ), we expect our ﬁndings to generalize 433 to these domains, although a more complete investigation to this end is left to future work. 434 Limitations While we explored how the LM performance degradation with time affects two types 435 of question-answering tasks, a broader variety of tasks is needed to obtain a more holistic picture on 436 how temporal generalization manifests in downstream tasks. An open research question is thus how 437 we can create and maintain benchmarks that are not static (Nie et al., 2020; Potts et al., 2020). 438 Is this all obvious? Our ﬁndings should not come us a surprise: That the world around us changes 439 with time—and thus what and how we talk about it also evolve accordingly—is hardly controversial. 440 But for the most part, these temporal dynamics are still not currently reﬂected in the way that we train 441 and evaluate our neural LMs. Our aim here is to highlight how such static evaluations overestimate 442 models’ performance, especially around predictions that relate to factual knowledge, which constitute 443 an important use case of NLP systems today. With the compilation of ever-larger web crawls for LM 444 pretraining (Gao et al., 2021), now is the right time to rethink how our splits are constructed (Søgaard 445 et al., 2021), construct temporal splits that evaluate models on their ability to generalize to future data, 446 and include timestamp information in both pretraining datasets and downstream tasks to facilitate 447 this kind of more realistic LM evaluation. This strategy will not only allow us to assess models’ 448 performance on a realistic type of out-of-sample data, but also circumvent test data contamination 449 affecting both LM and downstream task evaluations more broadly, e.g., in widely used QA datasets 450 like Natural Questions (Kwiatkowski et al., 2019) and TriviaQA (Joshi et al., 2017a), which have 451 been shown to contain alarmingly high proportions of overlapping training and test data (Lewis et al., 452 2020c). Our dynamic, streaming LM benchmarks—alongside the evaluation metrics that evaluate 453 LMs on things that matter the most for temporal generalization (e.g. named entities, EMERGING NEW 454 WORDS )—will be released to encourage more research in this area, and reward the development of 455 adaptive language models that can remain up-to-date with respect to our non-stationary world. 456 9
61: 10 545 547 11 12 13 662 663 , 664 665 666 667 Checklist 668 1. For all authors... 669 (a) Do the main claims made in the abstract and introduction accurately reﬂect the paper’s 670 contributions and scope? [Yes] 671 (b) Did you describe the limitations of your work? [Yes] See the “Limitation” part of 672 Section 8. 673 (c) Did you discuss any potential negative societal impacts of your work? [Yes] We work 674 with large-scale language models. In the paper, we have outlined risks with respect to 675 costs associated with keeping models up-to-date using brute-force retraining techniques. 676 As with prior work, other risks that stem from language modelling research on large 677 amounts of data may also apply to our work (Bender et al., 2021). 678 (d) Have you read the ethics review guidelines and ensured that your paper conforms to 679 them? [Yes] We have outlined the potential negative social impacts of our work in the 680 answer to Checklist 1.(c) above. 681 2. If you are including theoretical results... 682 (a) Did you state the full set of assumptions of all theoretical results? [N/A] This paper 683 does not include theoretical results. 684 14 (b) Did you include complete proofs of all theoretical results? [N/A] 685 3. If you ran experiments... 686 (a) Did you include the code, data, and instructions needed to reproduce the main exper- 687 imental results (either in the supplemental material or as a URL)? [Yes] All dataset 688 details and preprocessing steps are described in Section 2.1. While we do not release 689 the code, the experiment can be repeated with publicly available Transformer imple- 690 mentations. The dataset splits will be released publicly upon the completion of the 691 review period. 692 (b) Did you specify all the training details (e.g., data splits, hyperparameters, how they 693 were chosen)? [Yes] The dataset and splits are described in Section 2.1, and will be 694 released publicly upon the completion of the review period. The hyper-parameters and model details are described in Section 2.3. (c) Did you report error bars (e.g., with respect to the random seed after running experi- 697 ments multiple times)? [Yes] We have conducted signiﬁcance testing and also tested the robustness of our ﬁndings by replicating our experiments in different conﬁgura- 699 tions (e.g., with larger model sizes, in different languages, in different datasets, and in 700 different yearly splits of our datasets). 701 (d) Did you include the total amount of compute and the type of resources used (e.g., type 702 of GPUs, internal cluster, or cloud provider)? [Yes] To train and evaluate the models, 703 including hyperparameter optimization, we used approximately 186,000 TPU hours. In 704 each experiment, we used 32 TPUs for training and 1 TPU for evaluation. 4. If you are using existing assets (e.g., code, data, models) or curating/releasing new assets... (a) If your work uses existing assets, did you cite the creators? [Yes] We build our data 707 using existing datasets, which we cite in footnote 4. 708 (b) Did you mention the license of the assets? [N/A] We release dataset splits as lists of 709 identiﬁers pointing to original datasets. The licenses of the original datasets apply. 710 (c) Did you include any new assets either in the supplemental material or as a URL? [Yes] 711 We will be providing the URL after the review period. 712 (d) Did you discuss whether and how consent was obtained from people whose data you’re 713 using/curating? [N/A] We build all our datasets through publicly available datasets. 714 (e) Did you discuss whether the data you are using/curating contains personally identiﬁable 715 information or offensive content? [No] We work with publicly available datasets that 716 have been used in prior work. An analysis of whether, and to what extent, personally 717 identiﬁable information can be extracted from these publicly available datasets are 718 beyond the scope of this work. 719 5. If you used crowdsourcing or conducted research with human subjects... 720 (a) Did you include the full text of instructions given to participants and screenshots, if 721 applicable? [N/A] This work does not include any crowd-sourcing or research with 722 human subjects. 723 (b) Did you describe any potential participant risks, with links to Institutional Review 724 Board (IRB) approvals, if applicable? [N/A] 725 (c) Did you include the estimated hourly wage paid to participants and the total amount 726 spent on participant compensation? [N/A] 727 15
